1️⃣ Concept / Definition

Monolith:

Single, tightly-coupled application containing all business logic, UI, and data access.

Changes or deployments affect the entire system.

Microservices:

Independent, loosely-coupled services focusing on specific business capabilities.

Each service has its own database, API, and deployment cycle.

Monolith → Microservices Split:

Process of breaking a large monolithic application into multiple independent microservices for better scalability, maintainability, and fault isolation.
1️⃣ Concept / Definition

Monolith:

Single, tightly-coupled application with all business functions in one codebase.

Example in banking: Payments, Loans, Fraud Detection, Customer Management, and ATM Operations all in one deployable system.

Challenges: Hard to maintain, slow deployments, single point of failure, and difficult to scale specific modules.

Microservices:

Independent, loosely-coupled services focused on specific business capabilities.

Each service has its own codebase, database, and deployment lifecycle.

Example in banking:

Payment Service – Handles fund transfers and settlements

Fraud Detection Service – Monitors transactions for anomalies

Loan Service – Processes loan applications and credit checks

Notification Service – Sends transaction alerts, emails, SMS

Monolith → Microservices Split:

Breaking a monolithic banking system into independent, domain-focused microservices.

Focus: scalability, fault tolerance, maintainability, and faster deployments.

2️⃣ Why Split Monolith? – Banking Benefits
Challenge in Monolith	Microservices Solution (Finance Example)
Tight coupling of modules	Independent Payment Service, Loan Service, Fraud Service → deploy separately
Hard to maintain & update	Small codebase per service → faster fixes and feature releases
Single point of failure	Fault isolation → Fraud Service failure doesn’t crash Payment Service
Scaling entire app for one load	Scale only high-traffic modules (e.g., Payment Service during peak hours)
Slow development cycle	Parallel development by teams → ATM, Loan, and Notification teams work independently
3️⃣ Key Principles of Splitting (GS-Level)

Domain-Driven Design (DDD)

Split by business domains: Payments, Loans, Fraud, Accounts.

Single Responsibility Principle

Each service handles one specific capability.

Loose Coupling & High Cohesion

Services communicate via APIs; internal logic stays self-contained.

Database per Service

Each microservice has its own DB/schema to avoid dependency.

Well-Defined APIs & Contracts

REST/gRPC APIs for inter-service communication.

Incremental Migration

Migrate critical or high-traffic modules first → minimize risk.

