2️⃣ Why Split Monolith? – Banking Focus

A monolithic banking application has all business functions in one deployable unit: Payments, Loans, Fraud Detection, ATM Operations, and Customer Management. While simpler initially, it creates challenges as scale and complexity grow.

Splitting into microservices solves these problems.

1️⃣ Independent Scalability

Monolith Issue:

Scaling requires duplicating the entire application, even if only one module (e.g., Payments) is under heavy load.

Microservices Solution:

Scale only the high-traffic module.

Banking Example:

During salary day, Payment Service needs 5× more capacity, but Loan and Notification services do not.

2️⃣ Fault Isolation / Reliability

Monolith Issue:

Failure in one module can crash the entire application.

Microservices Solution:

Each service runs independently; failures are isolated.

Banking Example:

Fraud Detection Service fails → Payment Service continues processing transfers.

3️⃣ Faster Deployment & Updates

Monolith Issue:

Any change requires redeploying the entire application, slowing down feature delivery.

Microservices Solution:

Each service can be deployed independently.

Banking Example:

Update Notification Service to send SMS alerts → deploy independently without touching Payment or Loan Services.

4️⃣ Parallel Development

Monolith Issue:

Multiple teams working on the same codebase risk merge conflicts and delays.

Microservices Solution:

Teams work on separate services independently.

Banking Example:

Loan Team works on Loan Service, Payments Team works on Payment Service simultaneously.

5️⃣ Optimized Technology Choices

Monolith Issue:

Entire system uses a single tech stack, even if some modules need special optimization.

Microservices Solution:

Each service can use best-suited technology for its domain.

Banking Example:

Fraud Detection Service uses real-time streaming with Kafka + Python ML models, while Payment Service uses Java/Spring Boot for transaction processing.

6️⃣ Business Continuity & Compliance

Monolith Issue:

Harder to isolate data and audit specific processes.

Microservices Solution:

Each service has its own database and logging, making compliance easier.

Banking Example:

Loan Service stores loan application history separately → audit-ready for regulators.

GS-Level Summary Statement

"Splitting a banking monolith into microservices enables independent scalability, fault isolation, faster deployments, parallel development, optimized technology choices,
 and improved compliance. It allows Payment, Loan, Fraud Detection, ATM, and Notification services to operate efficiently,
 even under peak load, while minimizing risks associated with tightly-coupled systems."
